{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-39502",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/73bbd191451c2b7769ccd2903fd0e6c9da81a11853071bbfbed9711267903b9e.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$\n\nWe also see that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ And, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{1}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -{1}^{+}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {1}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {1}^{+}$\n\nThese give us the asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
